<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yalunka</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jalonke, Djallonké</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yalunka, or Jalonke, are a Mande-speaking people of the Futa Jallon highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, closely related to the Susu. They held the highlands, which take their name from them, until the Muslim Fulbe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of the early 18th century overthrew their chiefs and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of Futa Jallon. Many Yalunka then moved into northern Sierra Leone.</p></div>
